---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import micrograph from '../assets/micrograph.jpg';

/**
 * The narrative hinge between the hero's claim and the evidence below it:
 * why a surgeon, of all people, ends up writing about aging. The micrograph
 * appears here first as a specimen in a frame — Plate II — before the
 * thesis section enlarges it to full bleed.
 */
---

<section id="question" class="surface border-t border-line py-28 lg:py-36">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr] lg:gap-24">
      <div class="max-w-[58ch]">
        <p class="eyebrow">Figure 1 &middot; The question</p>
        <h2 class="hang mt-5 text-h2 text-fg">
          The question that started everything.
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-9 space-y-6 text-lead text-muted">
          <p class="resolve" style="--resolve-y: 1rem">
            Three decades of operating gives you a particular view of the
            body. You see, directly and repeatedly, what tissue does when it
            is damaged &mdash; and what it does when it repairs. The machinery
            is extraordinary, and for most of a life it simply works.
          </p>
          <p class="resolve" style="--resolve-y: 1rem">
            Then, in the same patient, it begins to falter. The question that
            follows a surgeon home is the one this book was written to answer:
            if the body can rebuild itself, why does it stop &mdash; and is
            that stopping negotiable?
          </p>
          <p class="resolve" style="--resolve-y: 1rem">
            <em>On the Origin of Species</em> asked how life began. This book
            takes up the question from the other end: not how life began, but
            where it is going, and whether its duration is fixed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <a
          href="/the-book/"
          class="group mt-10 inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 text-fg transition-colors hover:text-accent"
        >
          <span class="question-link">Read the full argument</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <figure class="resolve self-center" style="--resolve-y: 2rem">
        <div class="question-plate panel p-3">
          <Image
            src={micrograph}
            alt="Triple-stain immunofluorescence micrograph of endothelial cells: blue nuclei, green actin filaments, red mitochondria"
            widths={[480, 720, 1000]}
            sizes="(max-width: 1023px) 92vw, 36rem"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="w-full"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="label mt-4 flex items-center gap-3 text-muted">
          <span class="whitespace-nowrap text-metal">Plate II</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="h-px flex-1 bg-line"></span>
          <span class="whitespace-nowrap"
            >Endothelial cells &middot; the machinery in question</span
          >
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .question-link {
    background-image: linear-gradient(currentColor, currentColor);
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: left bottom;
    background-size: 0% 1px;
    padding-bottom: 2px;
    transition: background-size 220ms ease;
  }

  a:hover .question-link,
  a:focus-visible .question-link {
    background-size: 100% 1px;
  }

  /* The specimen is presented, not decorated: one hairline frame, and the
     stain colours doing the work. */
  .question-plate :global(img) {
    filter: saturate(112%) contrast(104%);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .question-link {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
